{"records":[{"id":"dby7kdtzk8","title":"Cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-Positive Diffuse Large B-cell Lymphoma, not otherwise specified: A systematic review and comparative analysis with Epstein-Barr Virus-negative, leg type","description":"Supplement 1. PRISMA flow diagram of literature search and study selection. \nEBV+ DLBCL, NOS, Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified; PRISMA, Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews and Meta-Analyses.\n\nSupplement 2. Histopathological features of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified. (A) Pleomorphic atypical large cells in a background rich in reactive cells such as lymphohistiocytes and plasma cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). (B) Monotonous sheets of immunoblast-like large cells (hematoxylin and eosin, x200). Atypical large lymphoid cells of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified highlighted by (C) MUM-1 (x200), (D) BCL-2 (x200), and (E) CD30 (x200); Epstein-Barr Virus infection detected by (F) positive Epstein-Barr encoding region in situ hybridization (x200).\n\nSupplement 3. Survival analysis of cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-positive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, not otherwise specified (EBV+ DLBCL, NOS). (A) Difference in overall survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and primary cutaneous Epstein-Barr Virus-negative diffuse large B-cell lymphoma, leg type (EBV- DLBCL, leg type). (B) Comparison in survival between primary cutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS and concurrent cutaneous and extracutaneous EBV+ DLBCL, NOS.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-01-27T07:21:33.083Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dby7kdtzk8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dby7kdtzk8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Woo Jin ","last_name":"Lee","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma","Lymphoma of the Skin"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"grnz4krxp2","title":"HHV-6A miR-aU14 and miR-30c alteration","description":"Raw data for the manuscript showing HHV-6A encoded miR-aU14 mediated miR-30c regulation.\nManuscript title: Selective inhibition of microRNA processing by a herpesvirus-encoded microRNA governs virus reactivation from latency\nSummary: Many viruses encode their own suite of miRNAs, which typically lack sequence homology to the miRNAs of their hosts. The molecular mechanisms and targets of virus-encoded miRNAs are incompletely understood. Here, we identify miRNA-mediated inhibition of miRNA processing as a general cellular mechanism that Human Herpesvirus 6A (HHV-6A) exploits to evade intrinsic host defense and drive the latent-lytic switch. We show that the most abundant HHV-6A miRNA (miR-aU14) selectively inhibits miRNA processing of miR-30 family members through direct RNA:RNA interaction. The subsequent loss of mature miR-30 results in a profound disruption of mitochondrial architecture via the miR-30/p53/Drp1 axis. This impairs type I interferon responses and promotes productive infection. Ectopic expression of miR-aU14 was sufficient and necessary to trigger virus reactivation from latency. Finally, we demonstrate that miRNA-mediated inhibition of miRNA processing represents a general mechanism in the cellular regulatory network that can be exploited to selectively target individual members of miRNA gene families.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-02-15T12:38:24.792Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/grnz4krxp2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/grnz4krxp2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bhupesh","last_name":"Prusty","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Virology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"5v545cw8t7","title":"Epstein-Barr virus BNRF1 destabilizes SMC5/6 cohesin complexes to evade its restriction of replication compartments. Yiu et al.","description":"Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) persistently infects most people worldwide. Delivery of ~170 kilobase EBV genomes to nuclei, and use of nuclear membrane-less replication compartments (RC) for their lytic cycle amplification, necessitate evasion of intrinsic antiviral responses. Proteomic analysis identified that upon B-cell infection or lytic reactivation, EBV depletes the chromosome maintenance cohesin SMC5/6, which has major chromosome maintenance roles and DNA damage repair. The major tegument protein BNRF1 targeted SMC5/6 complexes by a ubiquitin proteasome pathway dependent on calpain proteolysis and cullin-7. In the absence of BNRF1, SMC5/6 associated with R-loop structures, including at the viral lytic origin of replication, and interfered with RC formation and encapsidation. CRISPR analysis highlighted RC restriction roles of SMC5/6 components involved in DNA entrapment and SUMOylation. Our studies highlight SMC5/6 as a key intrinsic immune sensor and restriction factor for a human herpesvirus RC and have implications for the pathogenesis of EBV-associated cancers.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-05-03T06:55:14.952Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/5v545cw8t7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/5v545cw8t7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Stephanie Pei Tung","last_name":"Yiu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dvsrtknwh5","title":"Epstein–Barr Virus Immunoevasins, Associated Processes and Protein-Protein Interactions ","description":"This dataset contains the supplementary materials for manuscript entitled: A Database-Driven In Silico Approach to Identify Epstein–Barr Virus Immunoevasins, Associated Processes and Protein-Protein Interactions ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-02T15:30:59.401Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dvsrtknwh5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dvsrtknwh5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Norazlin","last_name":"Abdul Aziz","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Host-Pathogen Interaction"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nz66hxkjrd","title":"Mechanism of LINC00174 in NPC","description":"Nasopharyngeal carcinoma (NPC) is a common Epstein–Barr virus (EBV)-associated head and neck malignancy with high invasiveness and metastatic potential. Although long non-coding RNAs (lncRNAs) have been recognized as crucial regulators in cancer biology via competing endogenous RNA (ceRNA) networks, the function and regulatory mechanism of LINC00174 in EBV-positive NPC remain largely unknown.\nIn this study, we provide the first evidence that LINC00174 acts as an oncogenic lncRNA in EBV-positive NPC C666\u001E1 cells. Mechanistically, LINC00174 directly sponges miR\u001E3713 and relieves its suppression on TPX2, thereby promoting cell proliferation, invasion, and migration while inhibiting apoptosis. We rigorously validated this novel regulatory axis using qRT\u001EPCR, Western blotting, dual\u001Eluciferase reporter assays, CCK\u001E8, colony formation, Transwell, and flow cytometry.\nOur findings uncover a new molecular mechanism underlying NPC progression and identify the LINC00174/miR\u001E3713/TPX2 axis as a promising diagnostic biomarker and therapeutic target for EBV-associated nasopharyngeal carcinoma.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-04-15T12:20:47.791Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nz66hxkjrd","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nz66hxkjrd"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"yan","last_name":"li","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/05a7g7f24","name":"Hebei Provincial Department of Science and Technology","grant_id":"20251470"}],"categories":["Cell Proliferation","Cancer Biomarker","Nasopharyngeal Carcinoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y395v54y8k","title":"GPCR signaling contributes to immune characteristics of microenvironment and process of EBV-induced lymphomagenesis","description":"Natural killer T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous lymphoid neoplasm of natural killer or cytotoxic T cells in association with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. In the present study, we performed a comprehensive proteogenomic characterization of 112 NKTCL, incorporating genomics, transcriptomics, and deep-scale proteomics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-22T07:19:27.75Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y395v54y8k","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y395v54y8k"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lymphoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"33tm3dd9dw","title":"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery ","description":"Raw data deposited for \"FAME-CRISPR: Improved CRISPR-Cas9 Genome Editing via HDAC Inhibition and Engineered Virus-Like Particle Delivery\" by Djamshidi et al-Cell Reports Methods","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-25T15:53:30.358Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/33tm3dd9dw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/33tm3dd9dw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mahbod","last_name":"Djamshidi","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Sanger Sequencing","Immunofluorescence"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xnpzsx3729","title":"Dataset for 'Multiple LDLR family members act as entry receptors for Yellow Fever virus'","description":"Using CRISPR-Cas9 screen, we identified LRP4 as an entry receptor for Yellow Fever virus. By screening the whole LDLR family members, we further identified LRP1, and VLDLR also act as receptors for Yellow Fever virus. Data were generated by NGS, flow cytometry, qRT-PCR, H&E staining, Bio-Layer Interferometry. There are 5 main Figures and 10 Extended Data Figures. Data were obtained from multiple cell lines including 293T, Vero, HepG2, HAP1, K562 and Raji cells. C57BL/6J mice, AG129 mice and hFGR mice were also used in this project.","source":{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-03-16T19:37:38.721Z","url":"https://digitalcommonsdata.wustl.edu/datasets/xnpzsx3729","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xnpzsx3729"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhenlu","last_name":"Chong","orcid":"0000-0001-6195-3452"},{"first_name":"Sean","last_name":"Hui","orcid":"0000-0002-9870-6823"},{"first_name":"Xueer","last_name":"Qiu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sathvik","last_name":"Palakurty","orcid":"0000-0002-2222-4553"},{"first_name":"Alan","last_name":"Sariol","orcid":"0000-0002-3127-0543"},{"first_name":"Tomasz","last_name":"Kaszuba","orcid":"0000-0002-5967-4670"},{"first_name":"Michael N.","last_name":"Nguyen","orcid":"0000-0002-4184-7078"},{"first_name":"Pengfei","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Saravanan","last_name":"Raju","orcid":"0000-0003-3216-5574"},{"first_name":"Paige D.","last_name":"Hall","orcid":"0000-0002-2799-4506"},{"first_name":"Christopher A.","last_name":"Nelson","orcid":"0000-0001-8730-4768"},{"first_name":"Israel","last_name":"Baltazar-Perez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"David A.","last_name":"Price","orcid":"0000-0002-0751-9943"},{"first_name":"Paul W.","last_name":"Rothlauf","orcid":"0000-0002-0941-4467"},{"first_name":"James","last_name":"Crowe","orcid":"0000-0002-0049-1079"},{"first_name":"Sean P.J.","last_name":"Whelan","orcid":"0000-0003-1564-8590"},{"first_name":"Daisy W.","last_name":"Leung","orcid":"0000-0002-7189-9557"},{"first_name":"Gaya K.","last_name":"Amarasinghe","orcid":"0000-0002-0418-9707"},{"first_name":"Adam L.","last_name":"Bailey","orcid":"0000-0002-6560-9680"},{"first_name":"Daved H.","last_name":"Fremont","orcid":"0000-0002-8544-2689"},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Diamond","orcid":"0000-0002-8791-3165"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U01AI073755"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"75N93019C00062"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00fj8a872","name":"Office of the Director","grant_id":"OD029608"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"U19AI181960"},{"id":"https://ror.org/043z4tv69","name":"National Institute of Allergy and Infectious Diseases","grant_id":"75N93022C00035"},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","grant_id":"OD029608"}],"categories":["Microbiology","Virology","Infectious Disease"],"journal_issn":["14764687"]},{"id":"7gwtb7mgrr","title":"Genomic and Transcriptomic Characterization of NKTCL","description":"Natural-killer/T-cell lymphoma (NKTCL) is an aggressive and heterogeneous entity of non-Hodgkin’s lymphoma, strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. To identify molecular subtypes of NKTCL based on genomic structural alterations and EBV sequences, we performed multi-omics study on 128 biopsy samples of newly diagnosed NKTCL and defined three prominent subtypes.\n\n ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-04-02T03:32:10.22Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/7gwtb7mgrr","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/7gwtb7mgrr"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Weili","last_name":"Zhao","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genome Sequencing"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":130544,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":127,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":130544,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130001,"restricted":64,"embargoed":479},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2175},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1388},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":592},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":451},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":439},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":413},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":408},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.